tlings, the levels that settle before their driver's self-loop is cut, which crystal 62 measures as the entire distance between the truth (slope 1.25) and the reset cascade (slope 2.00). Measured this session (explorer/seeder_ladder.mjs, explorer/seeder_front.mjs): a ladder that uses this rule, the reset rule, and crystal 45's white branch and never consults the truth reaches level 5001 at index 8486 — slope 1.697, and its index never exceeds the wall's budget of 2k at any of 5000 levels — where the reset-only ladder runs at slope 1.989 and is refuted outright at k = 119 (crystal 62: C_119 = 237 > 236). The double rule fires 1398 times against 2201 single steps, and the two-rule ladder's worst ratio, 1.873, falls at level 118, the same event that breaks the reset-only ladder. Separately, on the true front at shift 16, every one of the 1872 early advances in the first 4000 levels is zero-cost — the front crosses two levels in one row or none — so this rule, and not a partial saving, is the shape the early settlings actually have.",","+    "disclaims": "Does not prove leftDiagonal_onset_le and does not bear on a prize conjecture — it is a claim about the left edge of the cone, where periodicity is already proved. It is one step rule and not a ladder: the ladder that would use it is deliberately not proposed, because a ladder's hypothesis shape is exactly what killed the previous one (leftDiagonal_onset_le_not_of_black_ladder), and 1.697 is a measured slope over 5000 levels, not a proof that the slope stays below 2. No witness is offered: the hypotheses are periodicity statements, which no finite range expresses, and reading leftDiagonal through evolve costs 3^(j+k), so a naive witness cannot reach index 18 where the first transient lives. The packed-row twin rowNat_return_succ_two carries the witness for the same mechanism instead.",","     "under": "leftDiagonal_onset_le",","     "route": {","-      "tactics": "  rintro ⟨N, hmono, hle, hwit⟩\n  have hge : ∀ k, k ≤ N k := by\n    intro k\n    induction k with\n    | zero => exact Nat.zero_le _\n    | succ k ih => have := hmono k; omega\n  have hEq : ∀ k, N k = k := fun k => le_antisymm (hle k) (hge k)\n  have h3 : leftDiagonal 3 3 = false := by\n    have := evolve_left_fourth_diagonal 3\n    simpa [leftDiagonal] using this\n  have h4 : leftDiagonal 3 4 = true := by\n    have := evolve_left_fourth_diagonal 4\n    simpa [leftDiagonal] using this\n  rcases hwit 2 with h | h\n  · rw [hEq 3, h3] at h\n    exact absurd h (by simp)\n  · have h5 := h 4 (by rw [hEq 2]; omega)\n    rw [h4] at h5\n    exact absurd h5 (by simp)",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.EvolveLeftFourthDiagonal"]","-    },","-    "witness": {","-      "expression": "(List.range 10).all fun j => leftDiagonal 3 j == decide (j % 2 = 0)",","-      "range": "j < 10 — diagonal 3 read to depth 12, which is the crux the proof instantiates (white at odd indices, black at even)"","+      "tactics": "  have h2 : PeriodicFrom (leftDiagonal (m + 2)) q (j + 1) :=\n    leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_step_of_black m q N j hNj h0 h1 hblack\n  refine ⟨h2, ?_⟩\n  refine bool_driven_periodicFrom_of_return\n      (fun i => leftDiagonal (m + 1) (i + 2)) (fun i => leftDiagonal (m + 2) (i + 1))\n      (leftDiagonal (m + 3)) q (j + 1) (j + 1) ?_ ?_ ?_ le_rfl ?_\n  · intro i\n    exact leftDiagonal_recurrence (m + 1) i\n  · intro i hi\n    have h := h1 (i + 2) (by omega)\n    dsimp only\n    rw [show i + q + 2 = i + 2 + q from by omega]\n    exact h\n  · intro i hi\n    have h := h2 (i + 1) (by omega)\n    dsimp only\n    rw [show i + q + 1 = i + 1 + q from by omega]\n    exact h\n  · have hb2 : leftDiagonal (m + 2) (j + 1 + q) = true := by\n      rw [h2 (j + 1) le_rfl]; exact hblack2\n    have e1 := leftDiagonal_recurrence (m + 1) j\n    have e2 := leftDiagonal_recurrence (m + 1) (j + q)\n    have hd : leftDiagonal (m + 1) (j + q + 2) = leftDiagonal (m + 1) (j + 2) := by\n      rw [show j + q + 2 = j + 2 + q from by omega]\n      exact h1 (j + 2) (by omega)\n    rw [show j + 1 + q = j + q + 1 from by omega, e2, e1, hblack2,\n      show j + q + 1 = j + 1 + q from by omega, hb2, hd]\n    simp",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.LeftDiagonalRecurrence", "Rule30.Proofs.LeftDiagonalPeriodicFromStepOfBlack", "Rule30.Proofs.BoolDrivenPeriodicFromOfReturn", "Mathlib.Tactic"]","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_of_rowNat_agree",","-    "lean_name": "leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_of_rowNat_agree",","-    "statement": "theorem leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_of_rowNat_agree (k p T : ℕ) (hT : T ≤ 2 * k)\n    (h : rowNat T % 2 ^ (k + 1) = rowNat (T + p) % 2 ^ (k + 1)) :\n    PeriodicFrom (leftDiagonal k) p k := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "The onset wall in one congruence per diagonal, and the interface every packed-row attempt on it wants. Diagonal k at index j is bit k of the packed row j + k (leftDiagonal_eq_rowNat_testBit, closed), so 'diagonal k has settled by index k' asks exactly that rows T and T + p agree on bit k for every T ≥ 2k. Agreement on the low k+1 bits at one time T ≤ 2k is enough, because the low bits are autonomous — see rowNat_agree_forward in this tier — and bit k depends on nothing above the residue mod 2^(k+1). Why a proof of the wall cites it: the wall follows in three lines by choosing T and p per k, checked as an `example` in explorer/seeder_scratch_front.lean, and this form is strictly freer than either closed reduction. leftDiagonal_onset_le_iff_rowNat_return pins T = 2k and p = 2^k; leftDiagonal_onset_le_of_stepMod_preperiod demands the preperiod bound for EVERY start x < 2^(k+1) when only the orbit of 1 is the seed's row. Here T and p are the prover's to choose. It is also the shape leftDiagonal_onset_le_of_le_5000 actually used to reach depth 5000 on the single shift 16, stated once so the next attempt does not rebuild it a fifth time.",","-    "disclaims": "Says nothing about whether such a congruence exists at large k; that is the wall itself, and what remains after this node is a lower bound on where the width-(k+1) truncated orbit of 1 first returns. The shift p must be allowed to vary with k and this statement does: no single p serves every k, because leftDiagonal_period_unbounded is proved and crystal 57 gives each uniform constant's exact expiry (16 dies at k = 87867). Not a prize conjecture; the left edge only.",","+    "id": "rowNat_return_succ_two",","+    "lean_name": "rowNat_return_succ_two",","+    "statement": "theorem rowNat_return_succ_two (n T p : ℕ)\n    (h : rowNat T % 2 ^ (n + 1) = rowNat (T + p) % 2 ^ (n + 1))\n    (hb : (rowNat T).testBit n = true)\n    (hc : (rowNat T).testBit (n + 1) = true)\n    (hd : (rowNat (T + p)).testBit (n + 1) = true) :\n    rowNat (T + 1) % 2 ^ (n + 3) = rowNat (T + p + 1) % 2 ^ (n + 3) := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "THE SAME DOUBLE ADVANCE WITH NO AUTOMATON IN IT, in the vocabulary the onset wall's own description tells a seeder to decompose through. The board's rowNat_return_succ_iff advances the agreement front between rows T and T+p by ONE bit per row, when bit n of row T is black. This advances it by TWO bits in the same row. Why it is true: bit n+2 of the successor row is bit n XOR (bit n+1 OR bit n+2), so if bit n+1 is black in both rows the two ORs are both true and bit n+2 of the successor is just the negation of bit n, which the hypothesis says already agrees. Cited by any packed-row onset argument, and this is the reason: leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_of_rowNat_agree needs the low k+1 bits to agree at some row T ≤ 2k, so the front must average better than one level per two rows, and rowNat_return_succ_iff alone gives exactly one level per black bit — a rate measured at 1.989 rows per level, which crystal 62 refutes at k = 119. This is the only rule on the board that moves the front faster than that. Its diagonal twin is leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_step_two_of_black; the two are the same fact in the two coordinate systems, and this one is the one a kernel computation can reach, since rowNat computes row 5000 in a second where evolve gives out near row 18.",","+    "disclaims": "Does not prove leftDiagonal_onset_le and does not bear on a prize conjecture. It is a sufficient condition, not a characterisation: the front also advances for reasons this rule does not name, and nothing here says how often the two black bits occur.",","     "under": "leftDiagonal_onset_le",","     "route": {","-      "tactics": "  have fwd : ∀ t, T ≤ t → rowNat t % 2 ^ (k + 1) = rowNat (t + p) % 2 ^ (k + 1) := by\n    intro t hTt\n    obtain ⟨d, hd⟩ := Nat.exists_eq_add_of_le hTt\n    subst hd\n    simp only [rowNat_mod_eq_iterate] at h ⊢\n    have e1 : T + d = d + T := by omega\n    have e2 : T + d + p = d + (T + p) := by omega\n    rw [e2, e1, Function.iterate_add_apply, Function.iterate_add_apply, h]\n  intro n hn\n  rw [leftDiagonal_eq_rowNat_testBit, leftDiagonal_eq_rowNat_testBit]\n  have hag := fwd (n + k) (by omega)\n  have key : ∀ m : ℕ, (rowNat m).testBit k = (rowNat m % 2 ^ (k + 1)).testBit k := by\n    intro m; rw [Nat.testBit_mod_two_pow]; simp\n  rw [show n + p + k = n + k + p from by omega, key (n + k + p), key (n + k), hag]",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.RowNatModEqIterate", "Rule30.Proofs.LeftDiagonalEqRowNatTestBit"]","+      "tactics": "  have hstep : ∀ t : ℕ, rowNat (t + 1) = rowStep (rowNat t) := fun _ => rfl\n  have hbits : ∀ r i : ℕ, (rowStep r).testBit i\n      = xor (decide (2 ≤ i) && r.testBit (i - 2))\n          ((decide (1 ≤ i) && r.testBit (i - 1)) || r.testBit i) := by\n    intro r i\n    have h4 : 4 * r = 2 ^ 2 * r := by norm_num\n    have h2 : 2 * r = 2 ^ 1 * r := by norm_num\n    unfold rowStep\n    rw [h4, h2, Nat.testBit_xor, Nat.testBit_or, Nat.testBit_two_pow_mul,\n      Nat.testBit_two_pow_mul]\n  have hiff : ∀ a b m : ℕ, a % 2 ^ m = b % 2 ^ m ↔ ∀ i < m, a.testBit i = b.testBit i := by\n    intro a b m\n    constructor\n    · intro hab i hi\n      have := congrArg (fun x => x.testBit i) hab\n      simpa [Nat.testBit_mod_two_pow, hi] using this\n    · intro hab\n      apply Nat.eq_of_testBit_eq\n      intro i\n      rw [Nat.testBit_mod_two_pow, Nat.testBit_mod_two_pow]\n      by_cases hi : i < m\n      · simp [hi, hab i hi]\n      · simp [hi]\n  have h2 : rowNat (T + 1) % 2 ^ (n + 2) = rowNat (T + p + 1) % 2 ^ (n + 2) :=\n    (rowNat_return_succ_iff n T p h).2 (Or.inl hb)\n  have hlow := (hiff _ _ _).1 h\n  have hlow2 := (hiff _ _ _).1 h2\n  rw [hiff]\n  intro i hi\n  by_cases hi' : i < n + 2\n  · exact hlow2 i hi'\n  · have hi2 : i = n + 2 := by omega\n    subst hi2\n    rw [hstep, hstep, hbits, hbits,\n      show n + 2 - 2 = n from by omega, show n + 2 - 1 = n + 1 from by omega,\n      hlow n (by omega), hc, hd]\n    simp",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.RowNatReturnSuccIff", "Mathlib.Tactic"]","     },","     "witness": {","-      "expression": "(List.range 7).all fun k => (List.range (2 * k + 1)).all fun T => (List.range 9).all fun p => !(decide (rowNat T % 2 ^ (k + 1) = rowNat (T + p) % 2 ^ (k + 1))) || (List.range 12).all fun m => decide ((rowNat (2 * k + m + p)).testBit k = (rowNat (2 * k + m)).testBit k)",","-      "range": "k < 7, T ≤ 2k, p < 9, conclusion read at 12 successive indices — entirely in the packed row model, so no 3^t cost and no depth wall"","+      "expression": "(List.range 200).all (fun T => (List.range 40).all (fun n =>\n  !((decide (rowNat T % 2 ^ (n + 1) = rowNat (T + 16) % 2 ^ (n + 1)))\n      && (rowNat T).testBit n && (rowNat T).testBit (n + 1)\n      && (rowNat (T + 16)).testBit (n + 1))\n  || decide (rowNat (T + 1) % 2 ^ (n + 3) = rowNat (T + 16 + 1) % 2 ^ (n + 3))))",","+      "imports": [],","+      "range": "T < 200, n < 40, at shift p = 16 — the real rows, read through rowNat rather than evolve, so it reaches far past row 18 where the first transient lives. Ran clean and returned true under lake env lean this session."","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "rowNat_agree_forward",","-    "lean_name": "rowNat_agree_forward",","-    "statement": "theorem rowNat_agree_forward (n T p t : ℕ) (hTt : T ≤ t)\n    (h : rowNat T % 2 ^ n = rowNat (T + p) % 2 ^ n) :\n    rowNat t % 2 ^ n = rowNat (t + p) % 2 ^ n := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "The low bits of a row are autonomous, so an agreement between two rows can never be lost. Bit b of row t+1 reads only bits b, b-1, b-2 of row t, so truncating to the low n bits commutes with the step; two rows agreeing there agree there for ever after. Three lines from the closed rowNat_mod_eq_iterate: the truncated rows are the orbit of 1 under stepMod n, so if two orbit points coincide at T, applying the map t − T more times keeps them coincident. Harvest rather than invention, and this is the argument for the node: it has now been re-proved privately three times, in three different sessions, under three names — the 'one autonomy induction' inside leftDiagonal_onset_le_iff_rowNat_return, `iterate_eq_of_eq` inside leftDiagonal_onset_le_of_le_5000, and part of `agree_bdry` inside the walk parked by onset attempts 11 and 12. It is the same case periodicFrom_trans_period was seeded on. Any proof of leftDiagonal_onset_le through the packed row cites it, because it is the only reason an agreement front is monotone in time, and monotone is what lets a front reach height k+1 by row 2k.",","-    "disclaims": "Pure bookkeeping about a deterministic map on finitely many states; there is no automaton content and no bound on anything. It says agreement is never lost, not that it is ever gained — gaining a bit is rowNat_return_succ_iff, already closed, and how often that succeeds is the wall.",","+    "id": "leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_of_rowNat_agree_any",","+    "lean_name": "leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_of_rowNat_agree_any",","+    "statement": "theorem leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_of_rowNat_agree_any (k p T : ℕ)\n    (h : rowNat T % 2 ^ (k + 1) = rowNat (T + p) % 2 ^ (k + 1)) :\n    PeriodicFrom (leftDiagonal k) p T := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "THE PERIOD WALL'S PACKED-ROW INTERFACE — the thing the onset wall was given on 2026-09-10 and the period wall still lacks. The landed leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_of_rowNat_agree carries the hypothesis T ≤ 2k, because it is aimed at the ONSET wall and must land the onset at k. leftDiagonal_period_le does not care where the repetition starts, only how long its period is, so that hypothesis is pure friction there and this drops it. With this in hand, leftDiagonal_period_le reduces to a statement with no automaton in it: for every k there is some p ≤ k + 1 and some row T with rowNat T ≡ rowNat (T + p) mod 2^(k+1). That is the period wall in the one vocabulary the kernel can compute in — rowNat reaches row 5000 in a second where evolve gives out near row 18 — and unlike the onset ladder's constant it carries no expiry: crystal 57 shows the onset route's constant 16 dies at k = 87867 because it is fixed, while here p is allowed to grow with k and the truth is about 2·log2(k), so the margin widens rather than closing. Cited by any period-wall argument that wants to work on rows instead of diagonals, and it is the companion to the closed leftDiagonal_period_le_of_white_count, which reduces the same wall to a count of eventually-white diagonals — two independent handles on one wall rather than one.",","+    "disclaims": "Does not prove leftDiagonal_period_le and does not bear on a prize conjecture. It is a strict weakening of the landed leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_of_rowNat_agree in its conclusion (onset T, not k) in exchange for dropping a hypothesis; it is not stronger, and it says nothing about how small p can be taken.",","+    "under": "leftDiagonal_period_le",","+    "route": {","+      "tactics": "  intro n hn\n  rw [leftDiagonal_eq_rowNat_testBit k (n + p), leftDiagonal_eq_rowNat_testBit k n]\n  have hmod : rowNat (n + k) % 2 ^ (k + 1) = rowNat (n + k + p) % 2 ^ (k + 1) :=\n    rowNat_agree_forward (k + 1) T p (n + k) (by omega) h\n  have hlt : k < k + 1 := Nat.lt_succ_self k\n  have e1 : (rowNat (n + k) % 2 ^ (k + 1)).testBit k = (rowNat (n + k)).testBit k := by\n    rw [Nat.testBit_mod_two_pow]; simp [hlt]\n  have e2 : (rowNat (n + k + p) % 2 ^ (k + 1)).testBit k = (rowNat (n + k + p)).testBit k := by\n    rw [Nat.testBit_mod_two_pow]; simp [hlt]\n  have hbit : (rowNat (n + k)).testBit k = (rowNat (n + k + p)).testBit k := by\n    rw [← e1, ← e2, hmod]\n  rw [show n + p + k = n + k + p from by omega]\n  exact hbit.symm",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.LeftDiagonalEqRowNatTestBit", "Rule30.Proofs.RowNatAgreeForward", "Mathlib.Tactic"]","+    }","+  },","+  {","+    "id": "stepMod_preperiod_of_return",","+    "lean_name": "stepMod_preperiod_of_return",","+    "statement": "theorem stepMod_preperiod_of_return (n N p x : ℕ)\n    (h : (stepMod n)^[N + p] x = (stepMod n)^[N] x) :\n    ∀ t ≥ N, (stepMod n)^[t + p] x = (stepMod n)^[t] x := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "THE MISSING ADAPTER BETWEEN THE TWO FORMS THE BOARD ALREADY SPEAKS. Every statement of the onset wall's arithmetic form that a computation or an induction can actually produce is ONE equation: leftDiagonal_onset_le_iff_stepMod_return is one equation per k, stepMod_preperiod_le_of_le_11 is one equation per (k, x), and crystal 61's universal form is one equation per (n, r). The interface that consumes them, leftDiagonal_onset_le_of_stepMod_preperiod, demands a ∀ t ≥ 2k statement instead. This is the two-line bridge: iterating one fixed map from a point that has already returned keeps returning, so one equation at N is the whole tail. Nothing composes those landed theorems today without it, and a prover who reaches the wall with a single return equation in hand currently has to rediscover it before the interface will take it.",","+    "disclaims": "Does not prove leftDiagonal_onset_le and does not bear on a prize conjecture. It is pure iteration algebra about an arbitrary function's orbit — it says nothing whatever about rule 30, and it is the only node in this tier that would be equally tru